Setting the level is per-transaction (or per-session) in most engines:

```sql
-- Postgres / standard SQL
BEGIN;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT ... ;
UPDATE ... ;
COMMIT;
```

```sql
-- MySQL: session-scoped, applies to the next transaction
SET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
```

At Serializable, both engines can raise a **serialization failure** (Postgres: `40001`, "could not serialize access due to concurrent update") when they detect a conflict that would violate serializability — the application is expected to **catch this and retry the whole transaction**. This is the real cost of Serializable: it's not slower per se, it's that correctness now depends on your retry logic existing at all.
